<title>Industry body takes a stand on ad-skipping and measurement</title>
<link>https://podnews.net/update/ppa-adskipping</link>
<pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 12:45:20 +0000</pubDate>
<podcast:transcript url="https://podnews.net/audio/podnews261125.mp3.vtt" type="text/vtt" rel="captions"/>
<description><![CDATA[A call to app developers to evaluate the downstream economic impact of their decisions.

Sponsored by Gamut Podcast Network. Want top 10 network reach and a team that actually emails you back? Gamut does both. If your show should be making more money, let’s talk. Email dan@gamutpodcasts.com or visit Gamut Podcast Network <a href="https://podnews.net/cc/3849">https://podnews.net/cc/3849</a>

Visit <a href="https://podnews.net/update/ppa-adskipping">https://podnews.net/update/ppa-adskipping</a> for the story links in full, and to get our daily newsletter.]]></description>
<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><i>Sponsored by <strong>Gamut Podcast Network</strong>. Want top 10 network reach and a team that actually emails you back? Gamut does both. If your show should be making more money, let’s talk. Email dan@gamutpodcasts.com or <a class="l" style="font-weight:bold;color:#BC1515" href="https://podnews.net/class-click/3849/2026-11-25">visit Gamut Podcast Network</a></i></p><ul>
<li><a href="https://podnews.net/press-release/ppa-advocacy-2026">Ad-skipping must balance innovation with economic sustainability</a>, says The Podcast Professionals Association, in a statement released today. Written by the organisation's Board, the advocacy piece - the first to be released by a major industry body - suggests that ad-skipping buttons are not inherently harmful, but encourages listening platforms and technology developers to communicate changes transparently, engage industry stakeholders, and evaluate the downstream economic impact of their decisions.</p>
<ul>
<li>In <a href="https://podnews.net/press-release/ppa-advocacy-2026">their article</a>, the PPA also calls for transparent measurement standards, suggesting that the AMP Accords offer a &quot;promising starting point&quot;. The nonprofit 501(c)(6) trade organisation also pledges its support for our Podcast Migration Code of Practice, joining large podcast hosting companies. It's the first industry group to do so.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>We're delighted to see podcast host iono<wbr>.fm as the latest podcast hosting company to support our <a href="https://podnews.net/article/migration-code-of-practice">Podcast Migration Code of Practice</a>. They're already supporting all of the recommendations. </li>
<li>Grand Theft Auto VI has podcasts in it, like <a href="https://podnews.net/update/monet-talks-libsyn#story8">this filthy one</a> - and <a href="https://www.greatpods.co/blog/gta-6-podcasts-who-voices-them-what-we-know-and-how-to-listen/?utm_source=podnews.net&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=podnews.net%3A2026-11-25">Great Pods has discovered who made them and what you can find in the game</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Podcasts everywhere are talking about GTA VI as well. In a clever use of data, <a href="https://transistor.fm/gta-vi-podcasts/?utm_source=podnews.net&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=podnews.net%3A2026-11-25">Transistor has highlighted 23 podcasts hosting on its platform that discussed GTA VI</a>, including Strauss Zelnick, the CEO of Rockstar Games.</li>
</ul>
</li>
